					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXAMPLE 5: PALETTE MODE\n\nSwitching to palette mode will change the input type from Color to Texture2d RGBA.\nThis means, you feed the Gradient node a texture, and the Gradient node will use that texture for its colors.\n\nIn this example we create two Linear nodes, which both produce a collection of 10 colors.\nWe combine the two collections with the Join node, which gives us a combined total of 20 colors.\nThis is converted to a texture using the Texture from Data node.\nThis texture is fed into the Gradient Node to produce 4 color Gradient.\n\nThere are many cool things you can do with collections to create unique gradients.\nExpand can be used to repeat the gradient multiple times or even mirror it.\nSort, Shuffle, Shift and Span can be used to move the gradient around.\nExperimentation is key!"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 790.96527099609375,
					"width": 1383.340087890625,
					"x": 280.39117431640625,
